@database "llm_router"
@author "Enrico Ehrich"
@$VER: llm_router.guide 1.0 (30.09.2026)
@smartwrap

@node main "LLM router"
@{h1}LLM router

This plugin lets an agent ask another model a question: it sends messages
to an LLM profile of @{tt}config/llm.yaml@{utt} -- one call, no tools --
and hands the answer back as a tool result. An agent running on a fast,
cheap model can get a second opinion from a stronger one, or the other way
round hand a bulk job to a cheap one, without a sub-agent. It has two tools,
no hooks and no panel, and it is off by default.

How models and profiles are configured is in
@{" LLM models and profiles " link scarabhive/llm}; this manual covers what
the router does with a profile.

@{code}
For agents and developers
  @{"  llm_router_chat  " link chat}
  @{"  llm_router_list_profiles  " link list}
  @{"  What the model sees  " link model}
  @{"  Setting it up  " link setup}
@{body}
@endnode

@node chat "llm_router_chat"
@{h1}llm_router_chat

Sends a conversation to the model behind one profile and returns its text
answer. The tool is named after the instance: @{tt}<instance>_chat@{utt}.

@{h2}What the called model gets

@{bullet}Exactly the messages given: no system prompt of its own, no tools,
nothing of the calling agent's conversation.
@{bullet}The profile's model entry as configured. The calling agent's
@{tt}llm_params@{utt} do not apply, and there is no fallback: a profile is
one model entry, and a failed call answers an error. (An agent's
@{tt}llm_profile@{utt} is a chain with fallbacks; the router's
@{tt}profile@{utt} is not.)
@{bullet}A new client for every call, built the way the core builds all
clients; a profile whose model entry is a batch model goes through the
batch queue when the batch system runs, and the call waits for it.

@{h2}Parameters

@{table}
Name | Meaning
profile | required; a profile name of @{tt}llm_system.profiles@{utt}. The schema lists them as an enum, but nothing checks the enum: an unknown name answers the error below
messages | a list of @{tt}{role, content}@{utt} objects, the fields of a chat message; content may be a string or a list of content parts. Wins over @{tt}message@{utt}
message | one text, sent as a single user message; used when @{tt}messages@{utt} is missing, null or empty
@{body}

Text content goes through the core's sanitizer, which removes null
bytes, control and zero-width characters; symbols, emoji and superscripts
stay. Content parts are passed on unchanged. Other arguments are ignored.

@{h2}Answer

@{code json}
{"content": "...", "profile": "fast", "provider": "openai_httpx", "model": "some/model-id"}
@{body}

@{table}
Field | Meaning
content | the model's text answer
profile | the profile asked for
provider | the @{tt}provider@{utt} of the profile's model entry in llm.yaml
model | the model id the client used
@{body}

@{h2}Errors

@{table}
Answer | When
No message or messages provided | neither argument, or both empty; a message that is not a string
messages must be a list of {role, content} objects | @{tt}messages@{utt} is not a list
messages[i] is not a {role, content} object | item i has no role, is not an object, or has a field of the wrong type
Profile parameter is required | no profile (checked after the messages)
Chat failed with profile 'x': Failed to create LLM client for profile 'x': Profile 'x' not found in LLM system profiles | an unknown profile
Chat failed with profile 'x': <error> | the client could not be built or the call failed; the error is the provider's
LLM routing request cancelled by user | the user cancelled the run; the answer also carries @{tt}cancelled: true@{utt} and @{tt}forced@{utt}
@{body}

Every error answer carries @{tt}error@{utt}; the two "Chat failed"
answers also carry @{tt}profile@{utt}. A cancel the user did not ask for (the task itself was
cancelled) is not answered: it ends the tool.

@{h2}Example

@{code json}
{"profile": "think", "messages": [
  {"role": "system", "content": "Answer in one paragraph."},
  {"role": "user", "content": "Is a B-tree or an LSM tree better for a write-heavy log?"}]}
@{body}
@endnode

@node list "llm_router_list_profiles"
@{h1}llm_router_list_profiles

Lists every profile of @{tt}llm_system.profiles@{utt} with the model entry
it points at. No parameters.

@{code json}
{"profiles": {"fast": {"description": "Everyday work", "model_ref": "my-model",
  "provider": "openai_httpx", "model": "some/model-id"}},
 "total_count": 1}
@{body}

@{table}
Field | Meaning
description | the profile's description, null when it has none
model_ref | the model entry the profile names
provider, model | from that entry; @{tt}unknown@{utt} and the model_ref when the entry is missing
@{body}

Without an @{tt}llm_system@{utt} section the answer is
@{tt}Failed to list profiles: Profile-based routing requires LLM system
configuration@{utt}.
@endnode

@node model "What the model sees"
@{h1}What the model sees

@{h2}The tool descriptions

They come from @{tt}schema.yaml@{utt}, rendered once when the server is
built:

@{code text}
llm_router_chat: Send messages to another LLM profile and return its answer (one call, no tools).
  profile: LLM profile name. Available: <names>. Each defines provider, model, settings.
  messages: Array of chat messages
  message: Single message text (alternative to messages array)
llm_router_list_profiles: List the LLM profiles with their model and description.
@{body}

The profile names appear twice, in the enum and in the description, so the
tool costs more tokens the more profiles llm.yaml has. The list is the one
the server was built with; a profile added to llm.yaml reaches it after a
restart.

@{h2}Status line

@{tt}Chat request using profile 'x'@{utt} while the call runs, then
@{tt}<n> chars via 'x' -- <model>@{utt}. A failed call shows its error.
@{tt}llm_router_list_profiles@{utt} ends with
@{tt}Retrieved <n> profile(s)@{utt}.

@{h2}Tokens and cache

The answer is an ordinary tool result appended to the conversation; the
plugin has no hooks and rewrites nothing, so the prompt cache is untouched.
The called model's tokens are not part of the calling agent's context --
only the answer is.

@{h2}Where the call shows up

The router hands the client's request reports to the
@{tt}pre_llm_request@{utt} and @{tt}post_llm_response@{utt} hooks as calls
without an agent, the way the decisions and speech clients report theirs:
the message debugger and OpenTelemetry see them, and the usage tracker
books their cost once, under the provider's name (openai_httpx, ...) with a
context window of 0, in the calling session. The calling agent's own
totals do not include them. This holds only for clients that report their
plain chat calls -- see the gaps below.

@{h2}Known gaps

@{bullet}Not every client reports a plain chat call to the hooks. The
batch client (a model entry with @{tt}provider: batch@{utt}), the
@{tt}anthropic@{utt} client and the @{tt}openai@{utt} SDK client (outside
realtime) never do, so a profile on one of them is neither seen nor booked.
The fix belongs to those clients, not to the router.
@endnode

@node setup "Setting it up"
@{h1}Setting it up

The plugin is off in the default configuration:

@{code yaml}
plugins:
  servers:
    llm_router:
      type: llm_router
      enabled: false
@{body}

Set @{tt}enabled: true@{utt}; an agent sees the tools when its allowlist
names them:

@{code yaml}
agent_config:
  tools:
    allowed:
      - "+llm_router/*"
@{body}

There are no server settings and no dependencies beyond the LLM provider
plugins the profiles use, and the plugin stores nothing. The profiles are
those of @{tt}llm_system.profiles@{utt}; which ones an agent can reach is
not limited -- every profile in llm.yaml is offered. SSL verification
follows @{tt}network.ssl_verify@{utt}.

@{h2}From the command line

@{code sh}
python -m plugins.llm_router --profile fast --message "Hello"
python -m plugins.llm_router --server --port 8081
@{body}

The first sends one message to a profile of the loaded configuration
(@{tt}--config@{utt}, else @{tt}config/config.yaml@{utt} or
@{tt}AGENT_CONFIG_PATH@{utt}) and prints the answer; it exits 1 on an error
answer. The second serves the tools over HTTP (default port 8081).
@endnode
